#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ft-eyes: our own eye tracker, live.

Reads the eye-camera frames that ft-eyegrab (the root service frametop-eyegrab, installed by
gaze/tracker/install.sh) keeps in /dev/shm/frametop-eyes-cams, finds each eye's pupil and
glint pair (eyes_pupil.py), turns them into a gaze with the saved calibration and a running
slip estimate (eyes_model.py), and publishes the result in /dev/shm/frametop-eyes-gaze,
where ft-gaze reads it as the source "own". It touches /dev/shm/frametop-eyes-want every
second, and ft-eyegrab copies frames only while someone does.

The gaze service (gaze/ft-gazed) runs it in the dev container, with --watch-stdin (it quits
when its stdin closes), while Eye tracker is Own tracker or the gaze probe uses it. The
probe calibrates and teaches it; the gaze pointer's nudges reach it as clicks, from ft-gazed.
By hand: distrobox enter dev -- python3 gaze/tracker/ft-eyes -v

Calibration: ~/.local/state/frametop/gaze/eyes/calibration.json, made by the gaze probe's
calibration with the tracker toggle on Own tracker (or lab/ft-eyes-score --save CAPTURE).
Each eye's shift since the calibration (its slip: slipfilter.py, a Kalman filter that clicks,
the glint pair and SteamVR's steady looks all teach) is kept in state.json next to it. After a
restart, or frames stopping for GAP seconds (the headset off), the slip is nearly unknown again
(SlipFilter.reseat): the next readings set it. eyeslip.py feeds and reads the filters, and
slip.jsonl there logs them (sliplog.py).

Control socket: abstract datagram "@ft_eyes"; each command gets one reply line.
  status                       JSON: calibration, and per eye its "shift" (feature units),
                               "clicks" (taught since the calibration, or since a reseat's
                               first), "reseat" (the headset was off and no click came since:
                               the probe asks for a one-dot check then), "slip_deg" (the shift
                               as the gaze error it causes looking ahead, yaw and pitch degrees,
                               fitcoach.assess's sign: the fit pill reads it), "slip_sd" (how
                               sure, degrees), and "glint_bias_deg", "steam_bias_deg"
  calib-start                  a new calibration: collect dots from now on
  calib-point T0 T1 YAW PITCH  a dot you looked at from T0 to T1 (CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW) in
                               that direction (head-relative degrees): "ok N0 N1 SD0 SD1"
                               (frames and spread in px per eye, right first), then
                               " (EYE eye only: WHY)" if one eye wasn't seen enough or moved
                               (the dot is kept for the other), or "fail WHY"
  calib-fit                    fit the dots, save, and start the shifts and clicks over. Each
                               eye is fitted on the dots it was seen at; one seen at too few
                               (eyes_model.MIN_CLICKS) is left out, and the other maps the
                               gaze alone ("..., left not used (9 dots, needs 12)")
  click T YAW PITCH            a click: you were looking there just before T; teaches the
                               shift: "ok DX0 DY0 DX1 DY1" (the shift each eye measured; nan
                               for an eye not seen, or when YAW PITCH lies outside that eye's
                               calibration dots by over eyes_model.EDGE_MARGIN: a shift measured
                               out there is the fit's extrapolation, so it teaches nothing).
                               Both eyes are judged together (slipfilter.teach_click)
  look T YAW PITCH             SteamVR's look, held still over eyeslip.LOOK_WINDOW before T (the
                               gaze service's steamlook.py): each eye whose pupil held still
                               then, within its dots, reads its shift against it, a weaker
                               reading than a click's: "look ok DX0 DY0 DX1 DY1" (nan as above)
  adjusting SECONDS            the headset is being moved on purpose for that long (the fit
                               pill is open): the shift may change fast. "adjusting ok"
  fit                          JSON: the last second of pupil finds per camera, for the fit
                               coach (gaze/fitcoach.py, Observations.summary); works uncalibrated
  finds start PATH | finds stop
                               append every frame looked at to PATH as JSON lines (the eye
                               recorder's own.jsonl): {"t", "cam", "x", "y", "a", "b", "angle",
                               "glints": [[x, y], ...], "gaze": [yaw, pitch] once calibrated,
                               "guard": true if the size guard refused it}, or {"t", "cam",
                               "lost": true}; with the net also "net": {"gaze",
                               "x", "y", "open", "visible"}. "finds stop" replies "ok ROWS"

The eye net (eyes_net.py) instead of the pupil finder: EYES_MODEL in ~/.config/frametop.conf (or
FT_EYES_MODEL), picked on Input Settings' Gaze page: latest, a version's name (gaze/tracker/models.json,
downloaded into gaze/tracker/models/NAME by models/catalog.py), or a model folder from frame-eyes'
training (anything with a "/"); classic or unset is the pupil finder. Each eye's raw gaze from the net
then stands where the pupil centre would: the calibration, the shifts clicks teach and the dots'
spread are in its degrees, and there are no glints (no slip estimate or jump notice). Each model
has its own state folder, net-NAME/ (calibration.json, state.json, clicks.jsonl), so switching
asks for a calibration once per model and the classic one is kept. Read at start: the gaze
service restarts ft-eyes when EYES_MODEL changes. If the model can't load, ft-eyes says
so and uses the pupil finder. While the fit coach asks ("fit") or finds are logged, the pupil
finder runs too, for them.

The pupil finder's size guard (eyes_pupil.SizeGuard): a find much smaller than that eye's usual
size, judged against how the other eye's size compares with its own (both narrow in light),
counts as not found. EYES_SIZE_GUARD in frametop.conf (or FT_EYES_SIZE_GUARD): both (the
default), long (against its own usual size only) or off. Read at start. Each eye's kept and
refused finds, and its usual size's restarts, are in the status ("size_guard_counts", since the
start), and once a minute that it refused any, a log line says how many ("size guard, the last
60 s: right refused 40 of 3100 finds; left refused 0 of 3200 finds").

The glint jump (eyes_model.Shift, EYES_JUMP) is gone from here since 2026-11-22: the slip filter
takes the glint estimate as one more reading. The lab tools still replay it.

Environment, for replays (lab/ft-eyes-e2e): FT_EYES_CAMS, FT_EYES_GAZE, FT_EYES_STATE (the
state folder), FT_EYES_SOCKET (the socket's name), FT_EYES_MODEL, FT_EYES_SIZE_GUARD. With
FT_EYES_CAMS set, the want file isn't touched.

/dev/shm/frametop-eyes-gaze, 128 bytes, little-endian (mirrored in ft-gaze.cpp):
   0 u32 seq      odd while it's being written: read it before and after, retry if it moved
   4 u32 version  1
   8 f64 t        the newest frame's time, CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW seconds
  16 f32 yaw, pitch  the gaze, head-relative degrees (yaw +left, pitch +up), eyes averaged
  24 u32 flags    bit 0: right eye in it, 1: left eye in it, 2: right shift taught by a click
                  since the calibration or the last reseat, 3: left
  28 u32 n        samples published
  32 f32 x4       right yaw, pitch, left yaw, pitch (NaN when that eye isn't seen)
  48 f32 x4       each eye's shift since the calibration, pixels: right x, y, left x, y
  64 f32 x4       pupil centre, pixels: right x, y, left x, y
"""
